182 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H3/P 123 0 R/T(Understanding the November 2026 Sunnyvale Ballot Measures)/E(Understanding the November 2026 Sunnyvale Ballot Measures)/Pg 13 0 R/K[3]>>
endobj

183 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 123 0 R/E(Ballots for the November 3, 2026 general election will arrive in mailboxes in early October. Lakewood Village is in Sunnyvale City Council District 6 \(represented by Council member Eileen Le\). Because only Districts 1, 3, and 5 are holding council elections this cycle, Lakewood residents will not see any City Council candidates on their ballot this year.)/Pg 13 0 R/K[4]>>
endobj

184 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 123 0 R/E(All Sunnyvale voters will, however, vote on three proposed changes to the Sunnyvale City Charter \(the city's constitution\): Measures G, H, and I. Each measure needs more than 50% of the vote to pass.)/Pg 13 0 R/K[5]>>
endobj

185 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 123 0 R/E(Note: As a nonpartisan 501\(c\)\(3\) organization, LVNA does not endorse or oppose candidates or ballot measures. This summary is provided solely for educational purposes to help neighbors understand the choices on their ballot.)/Pg 13 0 R/K[6]>>
endobj

186 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 123 0 R/E(Measure G: How the City Hires Builders for Public Projects)/Pg 13 0 R/K[7]>>
endobj

187 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 123 0 R/E(What it does: Right now, the city charter requires the city to hire designers and construction companies through separate contracts and pick whichever company submits the lowest bid. Measure G allows the city to also use modern contracting options, such as hiring a single team to handle both design and construction together.)/Pg 13 0 R/K[8]>>
endobj

188 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 123 0 R/E(Arguments in favor: Supporters say this gives the city more flexibility to finish complex public projects faster, reduce delays, and prevent cost overruns. The traditional lowest-bidder process would still be available when it makes sense.)/Pg 13 0 R/K[9]>>
endobj

189 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 123 0 R/E(Arguments against: Critics often prefer keeping the strict lowest-bidder rule to ensure direct price competition and reduce city staff discretion when choosing contractors.)/Pg 13 0 R/K[10]>>
endobj

190 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 123 0 R/E(Measure H: Payout Limits for Legal Claims)/Pg 13 0 R/K[11]>>
endobj

191 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 123 0 R/E(What it does: If someone files a legal claim or lawsuit against the city \(for example, property damage caused by a city vehicle\), the City Manager can currently approve payouts only up to $50,000. Anything higher requires a formal vote by the City Council. That $50,000 limit was set in 1989. Measure H allows the City Council to update this threshold, with plans to set it at $250,000.)/Pg 13 0 R/K[12]>>
endobj

192 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 123 0 R/E(Arguments in favor: Supporters point out that $50,000 buys much less today than in 1989 due to inflation. Raising the limit lets the city settle minor, routine claims quickly without adding them to busy City Council agendas.)/Pg 13 0 R/K[13]>>
endobj

193 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 123 0 R/E(Arguments against: Opponents prefer keeping the $50,000 limit so that elected council members publicly review and vote on all major payouts.)/Pg 13 0 R/K[14]>>
endobj

194 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 123 0 R/E(Measure I: Filling Empty City Council Seats)/Pg 13 0 R/K[15]>>
endobj

195 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 123 0 R/E(What it does: If a council member leaves office early and the City Council appoints a replacement, current rules require the city to hold a special election at the first available opportunity. Measure I removes that requirement, allowing the appointed person to serve until the next regular city election.)/Pg 13 0 R/K[16]>>
endobj

196 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 123 0 R/E(Arguments in favor: Proponents argue that special elections cost taxpayers hundreds of thousands of dollars to fill a seat for only a short period. Letting an appointee finish the term until the next scheduled election saves public money.)/Pg 13 0 R/K[17]>>
endobj

197 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 123 0 R/E(Arguments against: Opponents believe voters should choose their representatives at the ballot box as soon as possible, rather than having an appointed official serve an extended term.)/Pg 13 0 R/K[18]>>
endobj

198 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 123 0 R/E(Learn More: Read the official arguments, rebuttals, and analyses by the City Attorney in your Santa Clara County Voter Information Guide or online at the City of Sunnyvale Elections website \(https://www.sunnyvale.ca.gov/your-government/city-clerk/elections\).)/Pg 13 0 R/K[19 372 0 R 21]>>
endobj

